1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,760
Python 3.15 is out,

2
00:00:01,780 --> 00:00:03,860
it shipped in October and the latest version

3
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:06,440
3.15.2 is already here.

4
00:00:06,660 --> 00:00:09,080
And this release is not just speed tweaks,

5
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:10,400
there are new keywords,

6
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:13,740
new built-in types and a brand new profiler.

7
00:00:13,940 --> 00:00:16,160
I tested every feature in this video on

8
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:18,560
the real release, so you will see actual

9
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:22,120
output not slides. Here are 6 features you

10
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:24,140
will actually use. First,

11
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:26,520
let's install it. If you use uv,

12
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:31,480
it is one command. uv python install 3.15.

13
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:33,140
A few seconds later,

14
00:00:33,140 --> 00:00:35,560
you have Python 3.15.2.

15
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:38,740
No installer, no admin rights and your old

16
00:00:38,740 --> 00:00:40,600
versions stay untouched.

17
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:42,740
If uv is new to you,

18
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:44,940
my next video covers it from scratch.

19
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:47,460
Feature one, lazy imports.

20
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:48,720
Here is the problem.

21
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:50,300
This little command line,

22
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:52,860
tool imports eight modules at the top.

23
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:55,160
But when you just ask for the version,

24
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:56,560
it uses only one of them.

25
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:58,840
Python still loads all eight,

26
00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:01,280
every single run. In 3.15,

27
00:01:01,460 --> 00:01:03,540
you put the new lazy keyword in front of

28
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:06,420
the import. The module is only loaded the

29
00:01:06,420 --> 00:01:08,480
first time you actually use it.

30
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:11,000
Same code, same imports at the top of the

31
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:13,600
file. I timed both versions,

32
00:01:13,740 --> 00:01:17,520
30 runs each. The normal imports took 355

33
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:19,200
milliseconds to start.

34
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:21,440
The lazy version took 87,

35
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:24,160
that is four times faster just by adding

36
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:26,880
one word. This is huge for command line

37
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:29,040
tools and big applications.

38
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:31,180
Feature two, frozendict,

39
00:01:31,180 --> 00:01:32,560
a new built-in type.

40
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:34,640
It is a dictionary that cannot be changed.

41
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:36,040
Why would you want that?

42
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:37,420
Because it is hashable.

43
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:39,860
So you can use a whole settings dictionary

44
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:44,060
as a key in another dictionary or in a cache.

45
00:01:44,420 --> 00:01:47,080
Here I look up the cached connection with

46
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:49,880
a new frozendict that has the same settings.

47
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:52,820
And it finds it even with the keys in a

48
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:55,440
different order. You can still make a modified

49
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:57,400
copy with the pipe operator.

50
00:01:57,820 --> 00:01:59,920
But if you try to change it in place,

51
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:02,620
Python stops you with a TypeError.

52
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:04,760
Perfect for configuration that should never

53
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:06,220
change by accident.

54
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:08,200
Feature three, sentinel.

55
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:10,360
You have probably written this pattern.

56
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:11,880
You need a default value.

57
00:02:11,980 --> 00:02:14,520
That means nothing was passed and None will

58
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:18,020
not work because None might be a real value.

59
00:02:18,300 --> 00:02:21,080
Most people create a private object and

60
00:02:21,080 --> 00:02:22,220
compare against it.

61
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:24,600
Now there's a built-in for it.

62
00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:27,440
MISSING equals sentinel with a name.

63
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:30,200
Call get setting with no default and the

64
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:32,360
function knows nothing was passed.

65
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:35,780
Pass None and it treats None as a real value.

66
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:36,800
And when you print it,

67
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:38,080
you see a clear name,

68
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:40,640
not some random object address.

69
00:02:40,740 --> 00:02:43,980
Feature four, unpacking in comprehensions.

70
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:46,540
Say you have a list of lists and you want

71
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:48,960
one flat list. In 3.15,

72
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:52,000
you can write star batch for batch in batches.

73
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:55,680
It just works. The same idea works for dictionaries

74
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:58,440
with double star to merged them.

75
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:02,080
Run the same file on Python 3.14 and you

76
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:03,720
get a syntax error.

77
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,200
On 3.15, you get the flat list and the merged

78
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:09,840
dictionary. Feature five is quiet,

79
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:11,800
but it fixes a classic bug.

80
00:03:12,020 --> 00:03:14,020
When you open a file without saying which

81
00:03:14,020 --> 00:03:16,860
encoding to use, Python used to pick your

82
00:03:16,860 --> 00:03:19,200
system's setting. On Windows,

83
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:21,260
that was often an old code page.

84
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:23,180
So writing text like café,

85
00:03:23,180 --> 00:03:27,700
or an emoji or Telugu could crash or save

86
00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:29,660
garbage. From 3.15,

87
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:32,180
the default is UTF-8 everywhere.

88
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:35,380
Here I open a file with no encoding argument

89
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:37,640
and the encoding is UTF-8.

90
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:40,660
Your scripts now behave the same on every

91
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:43,220
machine. Feature six is my favorite.

92
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:47,140
Python 3.15 ships a new profiling package

93
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:50,120
with a sampling profiler called Tachyon.

94
00:03:50,380 --> 00:03:52,880
Here is a script that builds a report and

95
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:55,240
it feels slow instead of guessing,

96
00:03:55,460 --> 00:03:59,440
run python -m profiling.sampling,

97
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:01,200
run, and your script.

98
00:04:01,340 --> 00:04:03,380
It samples your program a thousand times

99
00:04:03,380 --> 00:04:05,800
a second with very low overhead.

100
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:08,760
The report shows slow total was on the stack

101
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:12,920
72 % of the time. And the culprit is JSON,

102
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:16,720
encoding and decoding every row for no reason.

103
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:20,840
Add dash dash flame graph and you get an

104
00:04:20,840 --> 00:04:23,620
interactive flame graph in your browser.

105
00:04:23,820 --> 00:04:26,840
One line fix, add the price directly.

106
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:32,080
The script went from 0.79 seconds to 0.24

107
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:36,300
more than three times faster found in seconds.

108
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:38,420
So that is Python 3.15.

109
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:41,020
Lazy imports, frozendict,

110
00:04:41,220 --> 00:04:44,520
sentinel, unpacking in comprehensions,

111
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:48,380
UTF-8 by default and the Tachyon profiler.

112
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:51,240
The full list is in the official what's

113
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:53,220
new page linked below.

114
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:55,360
Which one will you use first?

115
00:04:55,460 --> 00:04:56,560
Tell me in the comments.

116
00:04:56,760 --> 00:04:57,880
And in the next video,

117
00:04:57,980 --> 00:04:59,110
I will show you uv,

118
00:04:59,110 --> 00:05:02,700
the fastest way to set up Python in 2026.

